Rem ===================================================================
Rem tests/test-fuzz.shoddy -- a thousand commands nobody would type.
Rem
Rem   ./build.sh test     (runs this and the rest of tests/)
Rem
Rem The scenarios and the walkthrough both go where a player goes. This
Rem one goes everywhere else: words drawn at random from the game's own
Rem vocabulary, one or two at a time, thrown at whatever state the last
Rem one left behind. It asserts nothing about what the game answers --
Rem only that after every single command the game is still a game.
Rem
Rem That is a weaker claim than the other suites make and it catches a
Rem different kind of bug: the index that goes out of range, the accessor
Rem read off the end of a table, the state no rule expected. Those abort
Rem the mill rather than producing a wrong answer, so no assertion about
Rem output would ever see them.
Rem
Rem It is deterministic. The commands are drawn with the game's own
Rem generator, run from a separate seed that is threaded alongside the
Rem game and never touches the game's own stream -- so a failure names a
Rem run and a step that can be replayed exactly.
Rem
Rem Note on death: dying asks whether you want to be resurrected, and
Rem that is a read. Under ./build.sh test the suites run with their input
Rem closed, so the answer is no and the run simply ends there -- which is
Rem why a run reports how many commands it survived rather than assuming
Rem it survived all of them.
Rem ===================================================================

Include "harness.shoddy"

Rem ---- what may be typed -----------------------------------------------
Rem Every synonym of every motion, object and verb, minus five verbs that
Rem would end the run rather than test it: quit and suspend and resume
Rem read from the terminal, and seed and waste exist to manipulate the
Rem generator and the lamp for testing, which would make the run's own
Rem accounting meaningless.

Def OffLimits() As List Of String
    { "QUIT", "SAVE", "RESUME", "SEED", "WASTE" }

Def WordsOf(rows As List) As List Of String
    Flatten(Map(rows, Fn(r) => Words(r)))

Let FuzzWords = Flatten({ WordsOf(Motions()),
                          WordsOf(Objects()),
                          WordsOf(Filter(Actions(), Fn(v) => Not Contains(OffLimits(), Tag(v)))) })

Def PickWord(r As Number) As String
    Nth(FuzzWords, RandRange(r, Length(FuzzWords)) + 1)

Rem Half the commands are two words, which is where the parser's rewrites
Rem and its object-then-verb swap live.
Def Phrase(two As Boolean, w1 As String, w2 As String) As String
    If two Then
        w1 & " " & w2
    Else
        w1

Rem ---- what must still be true afterwards ------------------------------
Rem None of these is about the cave being sensible -- only about the game
Rem being a game: you are somewhere, you are carrying things that exist,
Rem the tables are the size they were, and the score is a score.

Def Sane(g As Game) As Boolean
    (Location(g) >= 0 And Location(g) < Length(Locations())
     And PreviousLocation(g) >= 0 And PreviousLocation(g) < Length(Locations())
     And LcgState(g) >= 0 And LcgState(g) < LcgM()
     And Length(Props(g)) = Length(Objects())
     And Length(ObjectLocation(g)) = Length(Objects())
     And Length(ObjectsAt(g)) = Length(Locations())
     And All(Held(g), Fn(o) => o > 0 And o < Length(Objects()))
     And NoneTwice(Held(g))
     And ScoreOf(g) <= MaxScore()
     And Turns(g) >= 0)

Rem You cannot be carrying the same thing twice, however you came by it.
Def NoneTwice(held As List Of Number) As Boolean
    All(held, Fn(o) => CountIf(held, Fn(x) => x = o) = 1)

Rem ---- the run ---------------------------------------------------------

Type Run
    Now  As Game
    Rng  As Number
    Went As Number

Def Fuzzed(r As Run, left As Number) As Run
    If left = 0 Or Not IsPlaying(Now(r)) Then
        r
    Else
        Let a = Rng(r)
        Let b = LcgNext(a)
        Let c = LcgNext(b)
        Let line = Phrase(RandRange(b, 2) = 1, PickWord(a), PickWord(c))
        Let g = Step(Hushed(Now(r)), line)
        Assert(Sane(g), "FUZZ BROKE THE GAME ON: " & line)
        Fuzzed(Run(g, LcgNext(c), Went(r) + 1), left - 1)

Rem Three runs from three seeds, from three different starting states:
Rem outside on the road, inside with the lamp lit, and deep in the cave
Rem where the curmudgeons are awake and the pirate is about.
Def CheckFuzz()
    Let onTheRoad = Fuzzed(Run(Fresh(), SeedX(1), 0), 150)
    Assert(Went(onTheRoad) > 0, "THE ROAD RUN TOOK COMMANDS")

    Let outfitted = Fuzzed(Run(Steps(Fresh(), { "in", "take lamp", "take keys", "light lamp" }),
                               SeedX(20250728), 0), 150)
    Assert(Went(outfitted) > 0, "THE WELL HOUSE RUN TOOK COMMANDS")

    Let deep = Fuzzed(Run(Steps(Fresh(), { "in", "take lamp", "light lamp", "xyzzy",
                                           "pit", "down" }),
                          SeedX(1494912171), 0), 150)
    Assert(Went(deep) > 0, "THE DEEP RUN TOOK COMMANDS")

    Rem A run that ends early ended in a way the game knows about -- it
    Rem was killed, or it won -- rather than in a state nothing can read.
    Assert(Ended(Now(onTheRoad)) And Ended(Now(outfitted)) And Ended(Now(deep)),
           "EVERY RUN ENDED IN A STATE THE GAME UNDERSTANDS")

    Rem A run cut short was killed, and the obituary it printed on its way
    Rem out is the stray text above this line. Saying how far each one got
    Rem turns that into information rather than noise.
    Print("     survived " & Str(Went(onTheRoad)) & ", " & Str(Went(outfitted))
          & " and " & Str(Went(deep)) & " of 150 commands")

Rem Playing, or one of the ways a game stops. There is no fifth thing a
Rem Fate can be, which is the point of it being a type.
Def Ended(g As Game) As Boolean
    Select Case Outcome(g)
        Case Playing
            True
        Case Quit
            True
        Case Died(m)
            True
        Case Suspended
            True
        Case Won
            True

Def Main()
    CheckFuzz()
    Print("ALL MUNGO CAVERNS FUZZ CHECKS PASSED")
